* Spin up an EC2 instance (`ex1`) using an Ubuntu AMI data source via `terraform apply`.
* Remove the EC2 instance from Terraform state management using the `terraform state rm` CLI command.
* Comment out the original resource definition in `resou.tf` using the Linux `sed` command.
* Re-import the active EC2 instance under a new resource name (`own_mport_ec2`) into `own_res.tf`.
* Define a `removed` block with `destroy = false` in `own_remove.tf` to declaratively untrack the resource.
* Resolve the "Removed resource still exists" validation error by commenting out the duplicate `resource` block in `own_res.tf`.
* Apply the declarative `removed` block to safely discard the EC2 instance from state without destroying it in AWS.
# Mastering State Decoupling: A Comparison of `terraform state rm` vs. `removed {}` Blocks

When managing AWS infrastructure with Terraform, there are times you want to stop tracking a live resource in code without actually deleting it from AWS.

In this lab, I created an EC2 instance, removed it from state management using the legacy CLI command (`terraform state rm`), re-imported it under a new name, and then safely detached it using the declarative `removed {}` block introduced in modern Terraform versions.

